      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The transition from a cluttered Dewey Decimal System to a scalable Library of Congress Classification involves a complex logistical overhaul, often requiring the physical relocation of stacks to accommodate the new, more granular call numbers. When DDC fails to scale, it creates \"shelf space\" conflicts where unique 19th-century manuscripts are forced into broad, generic categories, making them difficult to locate and often resulting in researchers digging through unrelated volumes to find a single specific diary.\n\nPractically, managing this transition requires a phased approach where the archives digitize the current DDC holdings to create a searchable metadata bridge while simultaneously re-shelving items according to the new LCC structure. The primary risks of a system that fails to scale include the loss of retrieval efficiency, where finding a specific manuscript could take hours instead of minutes, and the potential for data fragmentation, where related documents are scattered across different sections of the building, effectively creating a \"lost\" collection that no one can access.\n\nFurthermore, if the system cannot accommodate future acquisitions, the archive may face a situation where new materials are stored in temporary holding areas rather than integrated into the main collection, leading to a gradual degradation of the entire cataloging infrastructure. This lack of scalability can also discourage researchers",
